title: '? Not mended: a root that lost its editability to a switch does not get it back on switching back.'
pid: 832ee961-d456-499d-a9d5-ea3345cb0d5c
body: |2-
  Found by the reviewer, not reproduced. In a view of roots L and V,
  where V contains J, the user's fork of L, and J is drawn editable
  under V: switched to the user's, the root becomes J, and since J's
  occurrence under V already holds J's editability, 'point_occurrence_at'
  write-protects the root. Switched back, the root shows L but stays
  write-protected, without content, because only a root that was
  editable is extended, and nothing remembers why it was not; a search
  hit must stay write-protected, and looks the same. 0cde1222 had the
  same gap. A fresh view of those roots would make the root editable
  and the occurrence under V write-protected, so the better mend may be
  for a re-pointed root to take the editability from a deeper
  occurrence: a question of who holds editability, which is Drew's.
